 $\\mu$ with alternative $H_a$: $\\mu < \\mu_0$ produces $z = -1.5$. Which expression gives the p-value?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"P(Z $\\leq -1.5)$","feedback":"Correct! For a left-tailed test, the p-value is the area to the left of the observed test statistic: P(Z $\\leq -1.5) \\approx 0.067$."},{"correct":false,"text":"P(Z $\\geq -1.5)$","feedback":"The area to the right of $-1.5 ( \\approx 0.933)$ would essentially measure agreement with $H_0$."},{"correct":false,"text":"2P(Z $\\leq -1.5)$","feedback":"Doubling is only for two-sided alternatives."}],"multi":false,"hint":"A \"less than\" alternative is left-tailed.","explanation":""},"key":"LDu7joSGpd"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-01bb414fc741582ab547f3300c36b8c1.mjs","model":{"question":"In example 2, the p-value was 0.012. Which is the correct interpretation?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"If $\\mu$ were 250 ppm, the probability of a sample mean as far from 250 as 247 is (in either direction) would be 0.012","feedback":"Correct! If the mean concentration really were 250 ppm, there would be only a 0.012 probability of getting a sample mean at least 2.5 standard deviations away from 250 (in either direction)."},{"correct":false,"text":"There is a 1.2% chance that the mean concentration is 250 ppm","feedback":"The p-value is not the probability that $H_0$ is true."},{"correct":false,"text":"If $\\mu$ were 250 ppm, the probability of a sample mean of 247 or lower would be 0.012","feedback":"Since the alternative is two-sided, \"as extreme\" includes both directions, not just below."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Condition on $H_0 (\\mu = 250)$ being true, and remember the two-sided alternative.","explanation":""},"key":"SIxcgKvJMC"}],"key":"UFzPoDG9T4"}],"key":"wioezbMHWr"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Stating Hypotheses and Summarizing Data for a Mean","url":"/pages/p216-m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mea","grou